# TIME * INT with large products.
#
# Derivation (ed.3 table 30: MUL on TIME and ANY_NUM gives the TIME
# scaled by the number; ed.2 table 24 likewise; a duration is a single
# quantity, so T#700ms * 4 = T#2800ms however it is stored):
#   p4  = 700 * 4 = 2800 ms (always)
#   p44 = 71487 * 44 = 3145428 ms (always)
#   back: (t*n) - t = t*(n-1) holds for n >= 1, TRUE
# cycle 0: t=T#1500ms  n=2  -> p = 3000
# cycle 1: t=T#999ms   n=3  -> p = 2997 (999 ms * 3 = 2.997e9 ns > 2^31)
# cycle 2: t=T#71487ms n=44 -> p = 3145428
# cycle 3: t=T#250ms   n=1  -> p = 250
# cycle 4: t=T#0ms     n=7  -> p = 0
# An implementation computing the nanosecond part as a 32-bit int gives,
# on cycle 1, (999000000 * 3) mod 2^32 = ... and a wrong p.
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: t n
outputs: p p4 p44 back
status: conformant
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 table 30 (MUL on TIME); ed.2 table 24
---
0 t=T#1500ms n=2 => p=T#3000ms p4=T#2800ms p44=T#3145428ms back=TRUE
1 t=T#999ms n=3 => p=T#2997ms p4=T#2800ms p44=T#3145428ms back=TRUE
2 t=T#71487ms n=44 => p=T#3145428ms p4=T#2800ms p44=T#3145428ms back=TRUE
3 t=T#250ms n=1 => p=T#250ms p4=T#2800ms p44=T#3145428ms back=TRUE
4 t=T#0ms n=7 => p=T#0ms p4=T#2800ms p44=T#3145428ms back=TRUE
